  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "L1 gold-context records report measured human absolute oral bioavailability values predominantly in the 50–100% range, all above the F≥20% threshold: doxazosin 62–75%, ACT-1004-1239 53%, suvorexant 82%, ofloxacin 80–95%, tegafur ~100%, nebivolol 96% in extensive metabolizers, balcinrenone 52%, dovitinib ~75%, mirtazapine ~50%, and baloxavir marboxil high.",
      "card_ids": ["C01","C04","C05","C06","C08","C09","C10","C11","C12","C15","C17","C19","C21","C22"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L2 records from the same contexts add F values above 20% across species and formulations, including doxazosin 62–69% in humans and 24.4% in rats, dovitinib 73–77% in rat/mouse, tegafur 43.79–70.84% in dogs, and suvorexant sufficient oral bioavailability in rats.",
      "card_ids": ["C23","C26","C27","C40","C42","C60","C63","C64","C65"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L3 includes a direct oral-versus-IV comparison for tedizolid (oral AUC 26.1 vs IV 30.5 µg·h/mL; oral Cmax 2.25 vs IV 3.02 µg/mL), indicating high oral bioavailability for that reference; other L3 records show measurable oral Cmax/AUC for related molecules but mostly lack IV comparators.",
      "card_ids": ["C89","C107","C70","C80","C68","C75","C102","C82","C98","C84","C95","C105","C77","C100","C104"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "L4 adds favorable solubility/dissolution and absorption-context evidence: fexuprazan, a sulfonyl-pyrazole/difluorophenyl analog, is freely soluble at pH 4.0; PNU96391 GI absorption exceeded 90% in rats; verubecestat is BCS class 1 with very rapid dissolution; and several polar drugs (ofloxacin, levofloxacin, E2074, compound 4) have high aqueous solubility. No query-specific permeability or efflux data were added.",
      "card_ids": ["C116","C147","C146","C159","C124","C127","C163","C118","C120","C141"]
    },
    {
      "claim": "No affirmative threshold-relevant evidence supports F<20%; the only sub-20% records are nebivolol-specific (low in rats, as low as 12% in some extensive metabolizers, range 12–96%) and are not query-matched.",
      "card_ids": ["C31","C50","C52","C57"]
    }
  ],
  "new_evidence_assessment": [
    {
      "family": "L4 intestinal_absorption_permeability",
      "applicability": "moderate",
      "direction": "supportive",
      "decision_effect": "strengthened",
      "card_ids": ["C116","C147","C146","C159","C124","C127","C163","C118","C120","C141"]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct human or preclinical PK/bioavailability measurement for the query molecule.",
    "No query-specific oral AUC, Cmax, or IV-comparator data.",
    "No query-specific permeability, efflux, transporter, or first-pass metabolism data; L4 selected records are mostly solubility/dissolution and one fraction-absorbed study, not permeability assays.",
    "Reference contexts are structurally diverse and transfer is indirect; L4 record transfer likelihoods are moderate (~0.64–0.73).",
    "No dose, formulation, or species context is available for the query."
  ],
  "decision_summary": "L1/L2 gold-context records predominantly support F≥20%, with most measured human values in the 50–100% range. L3 adds a direct oral-versus-IV comparison for tedizolid and measurable oral exposure for related molecules. L4 adds favorable solubility/dissolution evidence, including a sulfonyl-pyrazole/difluorophenyl analog (fexuprazan) reported freely soluble and a near-complete GI absorption record, but no query-specific permeability or PK. The high prediction is retained and modestly strengthened; confidence remains moderate because transfer is indirect."
}

